  {
    "sha": "679380d5a73f7eef81572a2579f36c71479b5c14",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVmlK7doAKDY3OTM4MGQ1YTczZjdlZWY4MTU3MmEyNTc5ZjM2YzcxNDc5YjVjMTQ",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mikekinney@Mikes-MacBook-Pro.local",
        "date": "2026-11-09T03:19:54Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mikekinney@Mikes-MacBook-Pro.local",
        "date": "2026-11-09T03:19:54Z"
      },
      "message": "feat(turning): an optional insert zone on a lathe holder body\n\nA vendor holder mesh that models its insert plate read the plate's edge,\ncutting its own chip, as a holder rub. A lathe body may now carry an\ninsert zone: a box in the turning tool frame about the tip whose contents\nthe clearance bands leave out (each facet crossing a face keeps its\noutside part exactly). The zone must hold the tip, may reach along each\naxis no farther than the tool's body size (a grooving or form blade's\nhalf width along the spindle), never past 25.4 mm (the largest ISO\nturning insert), and may not hold the whole body - refused by name at\nSetToolBody, ChangeTool and on load.\n\nLatheBodySpec gains `zone`; SetToolBody::insert_zone sets or clears the\nzone on a tool's existing lathe body (undoable). On file it is an\noptional `insert_zone` key: no version rung, since an older reader skips\nit and over-reports (blocks), never passes a rub.\n\nFixes #396\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
